      {
        "subtitle": "Neurons are weighted sums",
        "description": "<p>A neuron multiplies its inputs by weights, adds a bias, and passes the result through an activation function. A layer is just many neurons evaluated in parallel.</p>\n<p>The <span style=\"color:#22d3ee;font-weight:600\">forward pass</span> is the process of turning inputs into predictions.</p>",
        "descriptionFormat": "html",
        "defaultLang": "py",
        "consoleBlock": {
          "c": "",
          "cpp": "",
          "go": "",
          "py": "import math\n\ndef sigmoid(x):\n    return 1 / (1 + math.exp(-x))\n\ninputs = [1.0, 0.5, -0.2]\nweights = [\n    [0.2, -0.4, 0.7],\n    [0.5, 0.1, -0.3],\n    [0.1, 0.8, 0.2],\n    [-0.6, 0.2, 0.4],\n]\nbias = [0.1, -0.2, 0.0, 0.3]\n\nhidden = []\nfor row, b in zip(weights, bias):\n    z = sum(x * w for x, w in zip(inputs, row)) + b\n    hidden.append(sigmoid(z))\n\nprint(\"hidden activations:\", [round(value, 3) for value in hidden])",
          "js": "",
          "java": ""
        }
      },
      {
        "subtitle": "Activation functions",
        "description": "<p>Without a non-linearity, stacked layers collapse into a single linear transformation. <span style=\"color:#facc15\">ReLU</span> keeps positive values and zeros the rest. <span style=\"color:#a855f7\">Sigmoid</span> squashes values into 0–1.</p>\n<p>The choice of activation affects gradient flow, not just the output range.</p>",
        "descriptionFormat": "html",
        "defaultLang": "py",
        "consoleBlock": {
          "c": "",
          "cpp": "",
          "go": "",
          "py": "import math\n\ndef relu(x):\n    return max(0.0, x)\n\ndef sigmoid(x):\n    return 1 / (1 + math.exp(-x))\n\nfor x in [-2, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 2]:\n    print(f\"x={x:>4}  relu={relu(x):>4}  sigmoid={sigmoid(x):.3f}\")",
          "js": "",
          "java": ""
        },
